[CHAPTER-4-DUAL-ISSUE-PAIRING]
UID: CH4-PAIRING

TITLE: Dual-Issue Pairing Rules

LOCATION: Chapter 4, Section 4.6, Table 4-1 (E200Z7CRM Rev. 2, page 4-19)

TABLE_4-1_CONCURRENT_INSTRUCTION_ISSUE_CAPABILITIES:
The manual shows explicit pairing rules in Table 4-1. Key rules:
- Scalar integer + Scalar integer: ✓ (Excludes divide class instructions occurring in both slots)
- Scalar integer + Scalar float: ✓
- Scalar integer + Vector integer: ✓ (Excludes vector MAC/multiply with scalar multiply/divide)
- Scalar integer + Vector float: ✓
- Scalar float + Vector integer: ✓
- Scalar float + Vector float: — (Cannot pair)
- Vector integer + Vector integer: ✓ (Excludes vector MAC/multiply in both slots, or divide in both)
- Vector integer + Vector float: ✓
- Vector float + Vector float: — (Cannot pair)
- Load/store can pair with most instruction types
- Branch can pair with most instruction types
- Special instructions cannot pair with anything

VALIDATION_STATUS: ✓ EXTRACTED FROM MANUAL
LLVM_CURRENT_STATE: 
- Code models dual-issue (IssueWidth = 2) but CompleteModel = 0
- Pairing rules partially documented in comments
- Resource conflicts used to prevent invalid pairings
- NEEDS UPDATE: Explicit pairing matrix should be extracted from Table 4-1

[CHAPTER-4-PIPELINE]
UID: CH4-PIPELINE

TITLE: Dual-Issue Pipeline Architecture

LOCATION: Chapter 4, Section 4.1 (E200Z7CRM Rev. 2)

NOTE: e200z7 has dual-issue capability with:
- Two dispatch ports (DIS0, DIS1)
- Two ALU units (ALU0, ALU1)
- MUL unit located in ALU0
- SPE/EFPU unit (Embedded Floating-Point Unit)
- LSU (Load/Store Unit)
- Branch unit
- 10 instruction prefetch buffers

VALIDATION_STATUS: ✓ MATCHES (based on code)
LLVM_CURRENT_STATE: Code correctly models functional units
- PPCScheduleE200Z7.td lines 22-41

[CHAPTER-4-TIMING]
UID: CH4-TIMING

TITLE: Instruction Timing Specifications

LOCATION: Chapter 4, Section 4.7, Tables 4-3 and 4-4 (E200Z7CRM Rev. 2, pages 4-20 to 4-24)

INTEGER_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Simple integer operations (add, sub, shift, rotate, logical, cntlzw): 
  Manual: 1 cycle latency, 1 cycle throughput
  Code: 1 cycle latency ✓
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:57-66
  
- Compare operations:
  Manual: 1 cycle latency, 1 cycle throughput
  Code: 1 cycle latency ✓
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:76-81
  
- Multiply instructions: 
  Manual: 3 cycles latency for result data, 4 cycles for record form conditions, 1 cycle throughput
  Code: 4 cycles latency ⚠️ (Should distinguish result data vs record form)
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:90-107
  DISCREPANCY: Code shows 4 cycles, but manual says 3 cycles for result data
  
- Divide instructions:
  Manual: 4-15 cycles (data dependent, worst case 15), blocking, 4-15 cycles throughput
  Code: 32 cycles latency ⚠️ (WRONG - should be 4-15, worst case 15)
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:82-88
  DISCREPANCY: Code shows 32 cycles, manual says 4-15 cycles (worst case 15)

LOAD_STORE_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Load instructions (non-multiple):
  Manual: 3 cycles latency, 1 cycle throughput
  Code: 4 cycles latency (LoadLatency) ⚠️ (Should be 3)
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:246
  DISCREPANCY: Code shows 4 cycles, manual says 3 cycles
  
- Store instructions (non-multiple):
  Manual: 3 cycles latency, 1 cycle throughput
  Code: Various latencies ✓ (should verify all are 3)
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:180-213

FLOATING_POINT_INSTRUCTIONS (SPE/EFPU):
- EFPU add, subtract, compare, multiply, multiply-accumulate:
  Manual: 4 cycles latency, 1 cycle throughput
  Code: Various latencies ⚠️ (Need to verify all match 4 cycles)
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:214-244
  
- EFPU divide and square root:
  Manual: 9 cycles latency, blocking (rest of pipeline unavailable)
  Code: Various latencies ⚠️ (Need to verify divide is 9 cycles)
  Manual location: Section 4.2.3, page 4-4

BRANCH_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Branch instructions:
  Manual: 6/4/1 cycles (worst case misprediction 6 cycles)
  Code: 1 cycle latency ✓
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:108-178
  NOTE: Manual shows variable timing based on prediction
  
- Branch misprediction penalty:
  Manual: Worst-case 6 cycles (from Table 4-3 and Figures 4-9, 4-12)
  Code: 3 cycles ⚠️ (WRONG - should be 6 for worst case)
  Code location: PPCScheduleE200Z7.td:271
  DISCREPANCY: Code shows 3 cycles, manual says worst-case 6 cycles

BYPASS_PATHS:
- GPR_Bypass: General-purpose register bypass
  Code: Modeled via E200Z7_GPR_Bypass
  Manual: Mentioned in pipeline diagrams (feed-forward paths)
  
- CR_Bypass: Condition register bypass
  Code: Modeled via E200Z7_CR_Bypass
  Manual: Mentioned in pipeline diagrams
  
- SPE_Bypass: Signal Processing Engine bypass
  Code: Modeled via E200Z7_SPE_Bypass
  Manual: Feed-forward paths mentioned
  
- FPR_Bypass: Floating-point register bypass
  Code: Modeled via E200Z7_FPR_Bypass
  Manual: Feed-forward paths mentioned

VALIDATION_STATUS: ⚠ PARTIALLY VALIDATED
- Key discrepancies identified in multiply, divide, load, and branch misprediction
- Manual chapter 4 timing tables extracted
- Current code has some incorrect latency values

[FUNCTIONAL-UNITS]
UID: FUNITS

TITLE: Functional Unit Specifications

LOCATION: Chapter 4, Section 4.2 (E200Z7CRM Rev. 2)

ALU0_AND_ALU1:
- ALU0 capabilities: Handles integer ALU operations, contains MUL unit
- ALU1 capabilities: Handles integer ALU operations (restricted subset)
- Manual: "Two execution units are provided to allow dual issue of most instructions"
- Manual: "Only a single integer divide unit is provided, thus a pair of divide instructions cannot issue simultaneously"
- Manual: "Divide unit is blocking"
- Code: E200Z7_ALU0 and E200Z7_ALU1 defined ✓

MUL_UNIT:
- Location: In ALU0
- Manual: "Multiply instructions have a latency of 3 cycles for result data and 4 cycles for condition codes for record forms, with a throughput of 1 per cycle"
- Code: E200Z7_MUL in ALU0 ✓
- Code latency: 4 cycles (should distinguish result data 3 vs record form 4)

SPE_EFPU_UNIT:
- Behavior: EFPU (Embedded Floating-Point Unit) operations
- Manual Section 4.2.3: "Adds, subtracts, compares, multiply, and multiply-accumulate pipelines have a latency of 4 cycles with a maximum throughput of 1 per cycle. EFPU floating-point divide and square root instructions have a latency of 9 cycles. While the divide is running, the rest of the pipeline is unavailable for additional instructions (blocking divide)."
- Code: E200Z7_SPE defined ✓
- Manual notes: Timing for SPE instructions is detailed in Section 6.3, "SPE Instruction Timing"

LSU:
- Load/store unit
- Manual: "When free of data dependencies, loads execute with a maximum throughput of 1 per cycle and a 3-cycle latency. Stores also execute with a maximum throughput of 1 per cycle and a 3-cycle latency."
- Code: E200Z7_LSU defined ✓

VALIDATION_STATUS: ✓ VALIDATED
- Functional unit descriptions match manual
- Need to verify latencies match manual specifications

[CRITICAL-DISCREPANCIES]
UID: DISCREPANCIES

DISCREPANCIES_IDENTIFIED:
1. Divide latency: Code shows 32 cycles, Manual says 4-15 cycles (worst case 15) ⚠️ CRITICAL
2. Multiply latency: Code shows 4 cycles, Manual says 3 cycles for result data (4 for record form) ⚠️
3. Load latency: Code shows 4 cycles (LoadLatency), Manual says 3 cycles ⚠️
4. Branch misprediction penalty: Code shows 3 cycles, Manual says worst-case 6 cycles ⚠️ CRITICAL
5. EFPU divide latency: Need to verify code has 9 cycles (manual Section 4.2.3)

ACTION_REQUIRED:
- Update PPCScheduleE200Z7.td divide latency from 32 to 15 (worst case)
- Update PPCScheduleE200Z7.td multiply latency to distinguish result data (3) vs record form (4)
- Update PPCScheduleE200Z7.td LoadLatency from 4 to 3
- Update PPCScheduleE200Z7.td MispredictPenalty from 3 to 6
- Verify EFPU divide is set to 9 cycles

[SPE-VS-FPU]
UID: SPE-FPU

TITLE: SPE vs FPU Configuration

LOCATION: Chapter 4, Section 4.2.3 and Chapter 5 (E200Z7CRM Rev. 2)

MANUAL_TERMINOLOGY:
- Manual uses "EFPU" (Embedded Floating-Point Unit) terminology
- Section 4.2.3: "Embedded Floating-point Execution Units"
- Section 5: "Embedded Floating-Point Unit"
- Manual mentions "SPE (SIMD)" in feature list (Figure 4-1)
- Section 6.3: "SPE Instruction Timing"

CURRENT_LLVM_CONFIGURATION:
- e200z7 defined with FeatureSPE (not FeatureFPU) in PPC.td line 542
- This appears consistent with manual terminology (EFPU/SPE)

VALIDATION_STATUS: ✓ VALIDATED
- Manual uses EFPU/SPE terminology, not traditional FPU
- LLVM FeatureSPE matches manual

